export default [
  { id: "coa1", category: "Pipelining", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is pipelining? What are the three pipeline hazards?", answer: "Pipelining overlaps instruction execution stages (fetch, decode, execute, memory, writeback) — while instruction 2 is in decode, instruction 1 is in execute, etc. This improves throughput (one instruction per cycle, ideally) without reducing latency. Three hazards: (1) Structural — two instructions need the same hardware (e.g., one memory port for fetch + data access) — fixed by duplicating hardware or stalling. (2) Data — an instruction needs a result not yet produced (e.g., add then sub using the sum) — fixed by forwarding/bypassing (route the result directly) or stalling (bubble). (3) Control — a branch changes the flow; the next instructions fetched may be wrong — fixed by branch prediction (guess and speculatively execute), delaying the branch resolution, or predication. Hazards cause stalls (bubbles — NOP cycles that reduce throughput). Deeper pipelines (more stages) increase throughput but worsen hazard penalties (longer flush on a misprediction) and branch misprediction cost." },
  { id: "coa2", category: "Pipelining", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is branch prediction? Why does a misprediction cost a pipeline flush?", answer: "Branch prediction guesses the outcome of a branch (taken/not taken, target address) before it's resolved — allowing the pipeline to fetch and speculatively execute the predicted path. If correct, no penalty. If wrong, the speculatively executed instructions are flushed (squashed), and the correct path is fetched — a penalty of (pipeline depth) cycles. Modern predictors: (1) Static — always not taken / backward taken (loops). (2) Dynamic — 1-bit (last outcome), 2-bit (two last outcomes — handles alternating branches better), two-level (history of recent branches — global/local), perceptron/tournament (ML-based). Accuracy: 95%+. The deeper the pipeline, the higher the misprediction cost (more stages to flush) — this is why branch prediction is critical for performance. Spectre exploited speculative execution (mispredicted branches access secret data before being squashed — the cache side effect leaks it)." },
  { id: "coa3", category: "Pipelining", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is data forwarding (bypassing)? How does it solve data hazards?", answer: "Without forwarding: instruction 2 (needs the result of instruction 1) must wait until instruction 1 writes back (5 stages → 3 stalls). Forwarding: the result is routed directly from the stage where it's produced (execute or memory) to the stage where it's needed (execute of the next instruction) — bypassing the register file writeback. Example: ADD R1, R2, R3 (produces R1 in execute); SUB R4, R1, R5 (needs R1 in execute next cycle) — forward R1 from ADD's execute output to SUB's execute input. This eliminates most data hazard stalls. Some hazards can't be forwarded (load-use: the load result is available in memory, but the next instruction needs it in execute — one stall, 'load-delay slot'). Forwarding requires extra data paths (muxes, comparators to detect when a register is being written and read simultaneously) — hardware complexity for performance. Combined with hazard detection units that insert stalls only when forwarding can't help." },
  { id: "coa4", category: "Pipelining", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a pipeline stall (bubble)? When is it needed?", answer: "A stall (bubble) inserts a NOP cycle — the pipeline doesn't advance for that instruction (it waits). Needed when: (1) Data hazard that can't be forwarded (load-use — the load result isn't ready until memory stage, but the next instruction needs it in execute — stall one cycle). (2) Structural hazard (two instructions need the same resource — stall one). (3) Branch resolution (if no prediction, stall until the branch is resolved). A bubble is a wasted cycle (no work done) — reduces throughput. The goal of pipeline design is to minimize stalls: forwarding (eliminates most data stalls), branch prediction (eliminates most control stalls), resource duplication (eliminates structural stalls). Stalls are inserted by the hazard detection unit (it freezes the PC and inserts NOPs). The deeper the pipeline, the more bubbles a hazard causes (longer to resolve). This is the tradeoff: deeper pipelines = higher clock rate (shorter stages) but higher stall/flush penalties." },
  { id: "coa5", category: "Cache", difficulty: "hard", question: "What are the three types of cache misses (the 3 Cs)?", answer: "(1) Compulsory (cold) — first access to a block (it was never in cache) — unavoidable (unless prefetching). (2) Capacity — the cache can't hold all the working set; blocks are evicted and later re-fetched. Fix: larger cache. (3) Conflict — multiple blocks map to the same set (in set-associative/direct-mapped); they evict each other despite free space in other sets. Fix: more associativity or victim cache. A fourth (the 4th C): Coherence — a block is evicted due to a coherence protocol (snooping invalidation in multi-core). Reducing misses: larger cache (capacity), more associativity (conflict), prefetching (compulsory), better algorithms (loop tiling for capacity). Compulsory misses are irreducible (the data must be loaded once); capacity and conflict are reducible by design. This taxonomy (Hill, 1989) helps diagnose cache performance." },
  { id: "coa6", category: "Cache", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is the difference between direct-mapped, set-associative, and fully-associative cache?", answer: "Direct-mapped: each block maps to exactly ONE cache line (index = block_address % num_lines). Simple, fast (one comparison), but high conflict misses (two hot blocks mapping to the same line thrash). Set-associative: blocks map to a SET of N lines (N-way: index = block_address % num_sets; the block can go in any of N lines in the set). N comparisons (parallel). Lower conflict misses. N=2,4,8,16 common. The sweet spot is 4-8 way (most conflicts resolved; more ways have diminishing returns). Fully-associative: a block can go in ANY line (no index — full search, or CAM). No conflict misses, but expensive (all lines compared in parallel — power and area). Used for TLBs (small, need flexibility) and victim caches. Direct-mapped is simplest but worst for conflicts; fully-associative is best but most expensive; set-associative is the practical compromise (most L1/L2 caches are 2-16 way)." },
  { id: "coa7", category: "Cache", difficulty: "hard", question: "How does a cache address split into tag, index, and offset? What determines each?", answer: "A memory address splits into: offset (byte within the block — log2(block_size) bits), index (which set — log2(num_sets) bits), tag (the rest — identifies the block within the set). Example: 32-bit address, 32KB cache, 64-byte blocks, 8-way: num_sets = 32KB / (64 × 8) = 64 sets. Offset = 6 bits (64 bytes), index = 6 bits (64 sets), tag = 32 - 6 - 6 = 20 bits. Lookup: use the index to find the set, compare the tag of all N ways in the set (parallel), if a tag matches and the valid bit is set — hit. The offset selects the byte within the block. The tag is stored per line (to compare). Larger blocks → fewer sets (smaller index, larger tag) — more spatial locality but more conflict (fewer sets). More associativity → fewer sets (smaller index) — less conflict. The split is determined by cache geometry (size, block size, associativity)." },
  { id: "coa8", category: "Cache", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a cache hit vs miss? What is the average access time formula?", answer: "Hit: the requested block is in the cache — served fast (1-4 cycles for L1). Miss: not in the cache — fetch from the next level (L2, L3, memory) — slow (10-100+ cycles). AMAT (Average Memory Access Time) = hit_time + miss_rate × miss_penalty. Example: L1 hit 1 cycle, miss rate 5%, miss penalty 100 cycles → AMAT = 1 + 0.05 × 100 = 6 cycles. Reducing AMAT: (1) lower hit time (simpler cache, smaller L1), (2) lower miss rate (larger cache, better algorithm), (3) lower miss penalty (L2/L3 cache, prefetching, out-of-order execution to hide latency). The memory hierarchy (registers → L1 → L2 → L3 → RAM → disk) trades speed for size/cost. Each level is ~10x slower but ~10x larger. The goal: make the common case fast (locality) and the rare case tolerable (caching, prefetching)." },
  { id: "coa9", category: "Cache", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a write-through vs write-back cache? What about write-allocate vs no-write-allocate?", answer: "Write-through: every write goes to both cache and memory — simple, memory always consistent, but slow (every write hits memory). Write-back: writes go only to the cache; the block is marked dirty; memory is updated when the block is evicted — fast (writes are cache-speed), but memory is stale (complexity for coherence — other cores must snoop the dirty bit). Write-allocate: on a write miss, load the block into the cache, then write (good if the block will be written again). No-write-allocate: on a write miss, write directly to memory (don't load the block) — good for write-once patterns. Combinations: write-through + no-write-allocate (simple, common in L1), write-back + write-allocate (common in L2/L3 — better for write-heavy workloads). Write-back needs a dirty bit per line (to know if memory is stale). Multi-core coherence (MESI) is built on write-back with dirty tracking." },
  { id: "coa10", category: "Cache", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is spatial vs temporal locality? How do they influence cache design?", answer: "Spatial locality: if an address is accessed, nearby addresses are likely accessed soon (arrays, sequential code) — exploited by larger cache blocks (fetch a block, not a byte — the nearby bytes are pre-loaded). Temporal locality: if an address is accessed, it's likely accessed again soon (loops, stack variables) — exploited by keeping recently accessed blocks in the cache (LRU replacement). Cache design: block size (spatial — bigger blocks prefetch more, but bigger blocks mean fewer total blocks → more conflict/capacity misses if the working set doesn't fit). Replacement policy (temporal — LRU keeps recently used blocks). Prefetching (spatial — fetch the next block before it's needed). Loop tiling/blocking (algorithm-level — restructure loops to fit the working set in cache, improving both). Locality is the fundamental principle that makes caches work — without it, caches would be useless (random access has no reuse)." },
  { id: "coa11", category: "Memory", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is the memory hierarchy? Why is it necessary?", answer: "Registers (ns, bytes) → L1 cache (1ns, KB) → L2 (3-10ns, 100KB-MB) → L3 (10-40ns, MB) → RAM (100ns, GB) → SSD (μs, 100s of GB) → HDD (ms, TB) → tape (s, PB). Each level is faster, smaller, and more expensive per byte than the one below. The hierarchy is necessary because: fast memory is expensive (SRAM for cache is ~1000x the cost of DRAM), and no single technology is both fast and large. The hierarchy gives the illusion of fast, large memory: the working set (actively used data) fits in the upper levels (cache), served at cache speed; the rest sits in slower levels. This works because of locality (temporal — reuse; spatial — nearby access). Without the hierarchy, every access would go to RAM (100ns) — 100x slower than cache. The OS and hardware cooperate: hardware caches (transparent), TLB (address translation), virtual memory (RAM as cache for disk), the OS manages page replacement. The goal: AMAT close to the L1 speed." },
  { id: "coa12", category: "Memory", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is the difference between SRAM and DRAM? Why use each?", answer: "SRAM (Static RAM): 6 transistors per bit, no refresh needed (data stays as long as power is on), fast (1-10ns), expensive, low density. Used for: CPU caches (L1, L2, L3), TLB. DRAM (Dynamic RAM): 1 transistor + 1 capacitor per bit, needs periodic refresh (the capacitor leaks — refreshed ~every 64ms), slower (50-100ns), cheap, high density. Used for: main memory (RAM). The capacitor in DRAM leaks charge — without refresh, data is lost. Refresh consumes power (a significant portion of DRAM power, especially when idle). SRAM is 6 transistors vs DRAM's 1 — SRAM is 6x the area per bit, hence expensive and low-density, but fast and no refresh. The tradeoff: SRAM for speed (cache), DRAM for capacity/cost (main memory). New technologies: HBM (high-bandwidth DRAM stacked near the CPU), persistent memory (Intel Optane — byte-addressable, non-volatile, between RAM and SSD)." },
  { id: "coa13", category: "Memory", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is the difference between a byte-addressable and word-addressable memory?", answer: "Byte-addressable: each byte has a unique address — address N refers to byte N. Most modern computers (x86, ARM) are byte-addressable — you can read/write a single byte. Word-addressable: each word (e.g., 4 bytes) has an address — address N refers to the word at byte 4N. Used in some older or specialized architectures. Byte-addressability is more flexible (you can access a byte without reading the whole word) but requires byte-enable signals (to write a single byte within a word). Endianness (big/little) matters for byte-addressable memory: a 4-byte int at address 0 — little-endian stores the least significant byte at address 0; big-endian stores the most significant byte at address 0. x86 is little-endian; ARM is bi-endian; network byte order is big-endian (hence htonl/ntohl)." },
  { id: "coa14", category: "Memory", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is endianness? What is network byte order?", answer: "Endianness: the byte order of multi-byte values. Little-endian: least significant byte first (at the lowest address). x86, ARM (default). Big-endian: most significant byte first. SPARC, PowerPC, network protocols. Example: 0x12345678 in memory — little-endian: 78 56 34 12; big-endian: 12 34 56 78. Endianness matters for: binary file formats (JPEG — big-endian; ELF — endianness-dependent), network protocols (TCP/IP headers are big-endian — 'network byte order'), and cross-platform data exchange. In C, use htonl/ntohl (host to network long) / htons/ntohs to convert. The confusion: when reading a hex dump, know the endianness. Bi-endian architectures (ARM, MIPS) can switch. The term comes from Gulliver's Travels (Lilliputians cracking eggs at the little vs big end). Most modern systems are little-endian (x86 dominance)." },
  { id: "coa15", category: "ISA", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is the difference between RISC and CISC? Is x86 RISC or CISC?", answer: "RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer): simple, fixed-length instructions, load-store architecture (only LOAD/STORE access memory; arithmetic on registers), many registers, simple addressing modes, hardwired control. Examples: ARM, MIPS, RISC-V. CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer): complex, variable-length instructions, memory-memory operations (arithmetic can access memory), fewer registers, complex addressing modes, microcoded control. Example: x86. The distinction has blurred: modern x86 decodes CISC instructions into RISC-like micro-operations (μops) internally — it's CISC on the outside, RISC on the inside. ARM has added some complex instructions (NEON, SVE). The RISC philosophy (simple instructions, pipelined, compiler does the work) won for performance; CISC's complex instructions are now decoded to RISC μops. RISC-V is a modern open RISC ISA. The key RISC advantage: pipelining and out-of-order execution are simpler with simple, regular instructions." },
  { id: "coa16", category: "ISA", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a load-store architecture? Why does RISC use it?", answer: "Load-store: only LOAD and STORE instructions access memory; all arithmetic/logic operates on registers. Example: `LOAD R1, [R2]; ADD R1, R1, R3; STORE R1, [R2]` — load, compute, store. Contrast CISC: `ADD [R2], R3` — adds directly to memory. RISC uses load-store because: (1) Pipelining — memory access is slow and unpredictable (cache miss); separating it from arithmetic keeps the pipeline simple (arithmetic stages are fast and regular). (2) Compiler optimization — the compiler can schedule loads early (hide latency) and keep values in registers (register allocation). (3) Simpler hardware — the execute stage doesn't need to access memory (one functional unit type). The cost: more instructions (load + add + store vs one add-memory) — but the simplicity and pipelinability outweigh this. Modern x86 (CISC) decodes memory-operating instructions into separate load + compute + store μops internally — effectively load-store at the microarchitecture level." },
  { id: "coa17", category: "ISA", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is an addressing mode? List common ones.", answer: "An addressing mode defines how the instruction specifies the operand's address. Common modes: (1) Register — operand in a register (ADD R1, R2). (2) Immediate — operand is a constant in the instruction (ADD R1, R2, #5). (3) Direct/Absolute — operand at a fixed address (LOAD R1, [0x1000]). (4) Register indirect — address in a register (LOAD R1, [R2] — R2 holds the address). (5) Base+offset — register + constant (LOAD R1, [R2 + 8] — struct field access). (6) Indexed — register + register (LOAD R1, [R2 + R3] — array access). (7) PC-relative — address relative to the PC (branches — position-independent code). (8) Auto-increment/decrement — register used then incremented (stack push/pop, array traversal). RISC has few modes (register, immediate, base+offset, PC-relative); CISC has many (x86 has ~24). More modes = compact code but complex hardware." },
  { id: "coa18", category: "ISA", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is the program counter (PC)? What is the instruction cycle?", answer: "The PC (program counter) is a register holding the address of the next instruction to fetch. The instruction cycle (fetch-decode-execute): (1) Fetch — read the instruction at the PC into the instruction register (IR); increment the PC (by the instruction length). (2) Decode — interpret the opcode and operands (which registers, what operation). (3) Execute — perform the operation (ALU, memory access, branch). (4) (Memory — if the instruction accesses memory). (5) Writeback — write the result to the register. Then repeat (fetch the next instruction). Branches/jumps modify the PC (change the flow). The cycle is the heartbeat of the CPU — each instruction goes through it. Pipelining overlaps the stages (fetch instruction 2 while executing instruction 1). The PC is also called the instruction pointer (IP in x86 — RIP in 64-bit). Interrupts save the PC (to resume later); the return address is the PC of the next instruction." },
  { id: "coa19", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is the difference between instruction-level parallelism (ILP) and thread-level parallelism (TLP)?", answer: "ILP: parallelism within a single instruction stream — multiple instructions of one thread execute simultaneously. Exploited by: pipelining (overlap stages), superscalar (multiple ALUs — issue 2-8 instructions per cycle), out-of-order execution (reorder instructions to fill slots), VLIW (compiler schedules parallel ops). Limited by data dependencies within the thread. TLP: parallelism across multiple threads — multiple threads run simultaneously (on multiple cores or with SMT). Exploited by: multi-core (separate cores per thread), simultaneous multithreading / hyperthreading (two threads share one core — fill each other's stalls). Not limited by intra-thread dependencies. ILP is hardware-extracted (the CPU finds it); TLP is software-provided (the program has multiple threads). Modern CPUs use both: ILP within each core (superscalar, OoO) + TLP across cores/SMT. ILP has diminishing returns (dependencies limit it); TLP scales with core count. GPUs are extreme TLP (thousands of threads)." },
  { id: "coa20", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is out-of-order (OoO) execution? How does it maintain correctness?", answer: "OoO: the CPU executes instructions in an order different from the program order — as soon as their operands are ready, even if earlier instructions are stalled (waiting for memory). This fills pipeline slots that would otherwise be bubbles (hiding latency). Correctness: the results are committed (retired) IN ORDER — a reorder buffer (ROB) tracks instructions in program order; instructions execute out of order but write their results to the ROB, and the ROB commits them in order (only when all earlier instructions have committed). If an exception occurs, the ROB flushes uncommitted instructions (precise exceptions — the state is as if the program ran in order). Register renaming eliminates false dependencies (WAR, WAW — two instructions writing the same register) by mapping architectural registers to physical registers. The CPU tracks true dependencies (RAW) and executes when ready. OoO is the key to modern CPU performance — it extracts ILP that the compiler can't (the CPU sees runtime values and latencies). The complexity: large ROBs, register files, reservation stations." },
  { id: "coa21", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is speculative execution? How did Spectre exploit it?", answer: "Speculation: the CPU guesses (branch prediction, memory disambiguation) and executes ahead before the guess is confirmed. If correct, the work is committed (free performance). If wrong, the work is discarded (squashed) — but the discarded work may have left traces in the cache. Spectre (2018): a mispredicted branch causes speculative execution that reads secret data (out-of-bounds array access) and uses it to access a cache line (the secret determines which line). The branch is resolved (misprediction), the speculative instructions are squashed — BUT the cache still holds the secret-dependent access pattern. The attacker times cache accesses (Flush+Reload) to infer which line was touched, leaking the secret byte. The fix: microcode updates, KPTI (kernel page table isolation), retpoline (replace indirect branches with safe sequences), and speculative execution barriers (LFENCE). Spectre is fundamental — speculative execution is core to performance; fully fixing it without huge performance loss is an open problem. Meltdown (a related attack) exploited that user-space speculation could read kernel memory (privileged mapping) — fixed by KPTI." },
  { id: "coa22", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is simultaneous multithreading (SMT / Hyper-Threading)? How does it differ from multi-core?", answer: "SMT: one physical core runs multiple threads simultaneously — the threads share the core's resources (ALUs, caches, register file — each thread has its own architectural state: PC, registers). When one thread stalls (cache miss, branch), the other thread uses the idle resources — improving utilization. Intel calls it Hyper-Threading (2 threads per core). Multi-core: multiple separate cores on one chip — each has its own resources (ALUs, L1 cache). True parallelism (two threads run simultaneously on two cores). SMT shares one core's resources (two threads compete for the same ALUs — if both are compute-bound, little benefit; if one is memory-bound, the other fills the stalls). SMT benefit: 15-30% throughput improvement (not 2x — resources are shared). Multi-core benefit: ~1x per core (true parallelism, limited by shared L3/memory). SMT is cheap (small extra hardware for the second thread's state); multi-core is expensive (full core). Modern CPUs: multi-core + SMT (e.g., 8 cores × 2 threads = 16 logical processors). Security: SMT shares the cache/microarchitectural state — side-channel attacks between threads on the same core (one thread can spy on another via the cache). Some security-sensitive deployments disable SMT." },
  { id: "coa23", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is Amdahl's law? What does it tell us about parallel speedup?", answer: "Amdahl's law: the speedup of parallelizing a program is limited by its serial fraction. If fraction p is parallelizable and (1-p) is serial, the max speedup with N processors is 1 / ((1-p) + p/N). As N → ∞, speedup → 1/(1-p) — the serial fraction dominates. Example: if 90% is parallel (p=0.9, serial=0.1), max speedup = 1/0.1 = 10x — even with infinite processors, you can't exceed 10x. If 99% is parallel, max = 100x. The lesson: the serial part (the part that can't be parallelized) is the bottleneck — you must minimize it. Gustafson's law (the counterpoint): if the problem size grows with the processor count (scaled speedup), the serial fraction shrinks — parallel speedup can grow. Amdahl is pessimistic (fixed problem size); Gustafson is optimistic (growing problem size). Real systems are between. The practical takeaway: identify and minimize the serial fraction (I/O, synchronization, sequential dependencies) — it's the limiter." },
  { id: "coa24", category: "I/O", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is memory-mapped I/O vs port-mapped I/O?", answer: "Memory-mapped I/O: device registers are mapped into the address space — the CPU reads/writes them with normal LOAD/STORE instructions. The same address bus and data bus serve memory and devices (a region of addresses is for devices). Used by: ARM, MIPS, RISC-V, and most modern systems. Pros: no special I/O instructions, all addressing modes work, easy in C (pointer dereference). Cons: consumes address space, must distinguish memory from device (the MMU or the bus decodes the address). Port-mapped (isolated) I/O: devices use a separate I/O address space, accessed with special IN/OUT instructions. Used by: x86 (IN/OUT on ports 0-65535). Pros: doesn't consume memory address space, clear separation. Cons: special instructions, limited addressing modes. Most modern architectures use memory-mapped I/O (simpler, unified). x86 supports both but uses memory-mapped for most devices (PCIe). The device registers (control, status, data) are at fixed addresses — the driver reads/writes them." },
  { id: "coa25", category: "I/O", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is DMA? How does it improve I/O performance?", answer: "DMA (Direct Memory Access): a DMA controller transfers data between a device and memory without CPU involvement per byte. Without DMA: the CPU does programmed I/O — reads a byte from the device register, writes it to memory, repeats — CPU-bound (can't do other work). With DMA: the CPU programs the DMA controller (source device, destination memory address, byte count), then the DMA controller transfers the data directly (cycle stealing — it grabs the bus when the CPU doesn't need it, or burst mode — it holds the bus for a block). The CPU is interrupted only when the transfer completes. Benefits: CPU is free during the transfer (high throughput), the device and memory transfer at their own speed. DMA is essential for high-bandwidth devices (disk, network, GPU). Modern devices have bus-mastering DMA (the device has its own DMA engine — no separate DMA controller). Scatter-gather DMA: the device transfers to/from non-contiguous memory regions (described by a descriptor list) in one operation — efficient for network packets and disk I/O." },
  { id: "coa26", category: "I/O", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is the difference between polling and interrupt-driven I/O?", answer: "Polling: the CPU repeatedly checks the device's status register (is data ready?) — busy-waiting. Wastes CPU (can't do other work), but low latency (immediate response). Used for: short waits, real-time, or when interrupts are too expensive. Interrupt-driven: the device signals the CPU (hardware interrupt) when ready; the CPU runs an interrupt handler. CPU is free during the wait. Higher latency (interrupt dispatch — save state, run handler, restore) but efficient for long waits. Polling is good for: high-throughput devices where interrupts per packet would overwhelm the CPU (NAPI in Linux — interrupt once, then poll for a burst). Interrupts are good for: unpredictable, low-frequency events (keyboard, disk completion). Hybrid: interrupt for the first event, then poll for a short burst (handles bursts without an interrupt per event). Modern network cards use interrupt coalescing (batch interrupts) to reduce overhead." },
  { id: "coa27", category: "Arithmetic", difficulty: "hard", question: "How does floating-point representation work (IEEE 754)? What is the format?", answer: "IEEE 754 float: sign (1 bit) + exponent (8 bits for float, 11 for double) + mantissa/significand (23 bits for float, 52 for double). Value = (-1)^sign × 1.mantissa × 2^(exponent - bias). The bias is 127 (float) or 1023 (double) — so the exponent is stored as an unsigned biased value. The mantissa has an implicit leading 1 (normalized — the 1 is not stored, giving 24 bits of precision for float). Special values: exponent 0, mantissa 0 = zero (±0). Exponent all 1s, mantissa 0 = infinity. Exponent all 1s, mantissa ≠ 0 = NaN. Denormals (exponent 0, mantissa ≠ 0) — no implicit 1, for very small numbers (gradual underflow). Precision: float ~7 decimal digits, double ~15. Rounding modes: round-to-nearest-even (default), toward zero, up, down. Issues: 0.1 can't be represented exactly (binary fraction), accumulation errors, NaN ≠ NaN (use isNaN)." },
  { id: "coa28", category: "Arithmetic", difficulty: "hard", question: "How does the ALU perform addition? What about subtraction?", answer: "Addition: binary add — full adders (sum = a XOR b XOR carry_in; carry_out = (a AND b) OR (b AND carry_in) OR (a AND carry_in)). Ripple carry: carry propagates from LSB to MSB (slow — O(n) gate delays). Carry-lookahead: computes carries in advance (group generate/propagate) — O(log n) delay, faster. Subtraction: two's complement — A - B = A + (~B) + 1 (invert B, add 1, then add). The ALU has an add/sub control: for sub, invert the B input and set carry_in = 1. Overflow detection: for signed, overflow if the carry into the MSB ≠ carry out of the MSB (or: adding two positives gives a negative, or two negatives gives a positive). For unsigned, the carry out indicates overflow. Multiplication: shift-and-add (or faster — Wallace tree, Booth encoding). Division: shift-and-subtract (restoring/non-restoring) or SRT algorithm. The ALU is the core computational unit; modern CPUs have multiple ALUs (superscalar) and separate FPUs (floating-point units)." },
  { id: "coa29", category: "Arithmetic", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is two's complement? Why is it used for signed integers?", answer: "Two's complement: to negate a number, invert all bits and add 1. For n bits, the range is -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1)-1 (one more negative than positive — e.g., 8-bit: -128 to 127). Advantages: (1) One representation for zero (unlike sign-magnitude, which has +0 and -0). (2) Addition works for both signed and unsigned (the same hardware — the CPU doesn't know if the numbers are signed; it just adds; the interpretation differs). (3) Subtraction is easy (A - B = A + (-B) = A + ~B + 1). (4) No special handling for signs (sign-magnitude requires separate sign logic). The MSB is the sign bit (0 = positive, 1 = negative). Overflow: adding two positives giving a negative (or two negatives giving a positive) — the CPU sets the overflow flag. Two's complement is universal for signed integer arithmetic. The asymmetry (-128 but no +128 in 8-bit) is a known quirk — abs(INT_MIN) overflows." },
  { id: "coa30", category: "Arithmetic", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is integer overflow? Is it the same in C and Java?", answer: "Integer overflow: the result exceeds the representable range. For unsigned, it wraps (modular arithmetic — defined behavior). For signed, in C it's UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR (the compiler can assume it doesn't happen — enabling optimizations that can produce surprising results). In Java, signed overflow wraps (defined — Java has no unsigned int except via methods). In Python, ints are arbitrary precision (no overflow — they grow). Signed overflow examples: INT_MAX + 1 → INT_MIN (wrap, in Java) or UB (in C). The difference matters: C's UB means the compiler can delete overflow checks (it assumes overflow doesn't happen) — a security issue (overflow checks optimized away). Rust panics on overflow in debug mode (wraps in release, or use checked_* methods). To detect overflow in C: use __builtin_add_overflow (GCC/Clang) or check before (if (a > INT_MAX - b)). For unsigned, the wrap is defined and sometimes used intentionally (hashing, counters)." },
  { id: "coa31", category: "Control", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is microprogramming (microcode)? How does it differ from hardwired control?", answer: "The control unit generates control signals for each instruction. Hardwired: the control signals are generated by combinational logic (gates, FSM) — fast (direct), inflexible (changing the ISA requires redesigning the hardware), used in RISC (simple instructions). Microcoded (microprogrammed): each instruction is implemented as a sequence of microinstructions (microcode) stored in a ROM (control store) — each microinstruction generates a set of control signals. Flexible (update microcode to fix bugs or add instructions — Intel ships microcode updates), slower (ROM read per microinstruction), used in CISC (complex instructions). Modern x86: the front-end decodes CISC instructions into μops (micro-operations) — some are hardwired (simple), some are microcoded (complex). Microcode updates (via BIOS/OS) can fix CPU bugs (e.g., Spectre mitigations) without hardware changes. The tradeoff: hardwired is fast and simple (RISC); microcoded is flexible and compact (CISC) but slower. Modern CPUs use a hybrid: simple instructions hardwired, complex microcoded." },
  { id: "coa32", category: "Control", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is an interrupt? What is the difference between a trap and a fault?", answer: "An interrupt is a signal that causes the CPU to suspend the current instruction, save its state (PC, flags), and run an interrupt handler (at a vector address), then resume. Types: (1) Hardware interrupt — from a device (timer, disk, keyboard) — asynchronous (happens anytime). (2) Software interrupt/exception — from the CPU itself — synchronous (during an instruction). Exceptions: Trap — intentional (syscall, INT instruction) — the handler runs, then returns to the NEXT instruction. Fault — correctable (page fault — the OS loads the page, then re-executes the faulting instruction). Abort — uncorrectable (machine check, double fault) — the process/OS is terminated. The distinction: trap resumes after the instruction; fault re-executes the instruction; abort doesn't resume. The interrupt vector table (IVT / IDT in x86) maps interrupt numbers to handler addresses. The CPU saves the PC (and flags) so it can resume. Interrupts are the basis of: I/O (device notification), syscalls (user → kernel), exceptions (page faults, division by zero), timers (scheduling)." },
  { id: "coa33", category: "Memory", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is virtual memory? How does the MMU translate addresses?", answer: "Virtual memory: each process has a virtual address space (e.g., 48-bit = 256TB) mapped to physical RAM (and disk for overflow). The MMU (Memory Management Unit) translates virtual to physical addresses on every access. Paging: the virtual space is divided into pages (4KB); physical memory into frames. A page table (per process) maps page numbers to frame numbers. Multi-level page tables (x86-64: 4 levels — PML4, PDPT, PD, PT) avoid allocating a flat table for the entire space (sparse — only used pages have entries). Translation: the MMU walks the page table (4 memory accesses for a 4-level table) — slow. The TLB caches recent translations (the TLB hit avoids the walk). A page fault (the page isn't in RAM) triggers the OS to load it from disk (swap), possibly evicting another page. Benefits: isolation (each process has its own page table), memory > RAM (swap), lazy loading, copy-on-write (fork). The page table entry (PTE) has: frame number, present bit, dirty bit, accessed bit, permission bits (read/write/execute), user/supervisor bit." },
  { id: "coa34", category: "Memory", difficulty: "hard", question: "What is a TLB? What is a TLB miss?", answer: "The TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer) is a cache of recent virtual→physical page translations (PTEs). Without it, every memory access would require a page table walk (multiple memory accesses — 4 for a 4-level table) — devastating for performance. The TLB is small (64-2048 entries) but fast (1 cycle). A TLB hit: the translation is in the TLB — use it directly (no walk). A TLB miss: the translation isn't in the TLB — the MMU walks the page table (hardware page table walk, or a software handler for TLB miss). The walk loads the PTE into the TLB (and the access proceeds). TLB misses are expensive (4 memory accesses for the walk) — the TLB is critical for performance. Ways to reduce TLB misses: (1) larger pages (2MB/1GB huge pages — one TLB entry covers more memory). (2) Better TLB coverage (larger TLB). (3) Page coloring (align virtual and physical pages to reduce conflicts). On a context switch, the TLB must be flushed (stale translations for the old process) — expensive. ASIDs (Address Space IDs) tag TLB entries by process to avoid flushing. PCID (Intel) is an ASID." },
  { id: "coa35", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is cache coherence? What is the MESI protocol?", answer: "In a multi-core system, each core has its own L1/L2 cache. If core 1 modifies a variable in its cache, core 2's cache has a stale copy — cache incoherence. Cache coherence protocols ensure all cores see a consistent value. MESI (Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid) — the states a cache line can be in: (1) Modified — the core has the only copy, and it's dirty (memory is stale). (2) Exclusive — the core has the only copy, and it's clean (memory is up to date). (3) Shared — multiple cores have the line, all clean (read-only effectively — a write triggers an invalidation). (4) Invalid — the line is not in the cache. Transitions: on a read miss, the line is fetched (Shared if others have it, Exclusive if not). On a write, the core sends an invalidation (other cores' copies become Invalid), then the line is Modified. Snooping: cores listen to the bus for reads/writes to maintain the state. MESI is the basis for x86/ARM coherence. MOESI adds an Owned state (shared but dirty — can supply the data without writing to memory)." },
  { id: "coa36", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a memory consistency model? What is sequential consistency?", answer: "A memory consistency model defines the order in which memory operations appear to execute — the guarantees the hardware provides to programmers. Sequential consistency (Lamport, 1979): 'the result of any execution is the same as if the operations of all processors were executed in some sequential order, and the operations of each individual processor appear in the order specified by its program.' Intuitive but expensive (no reordering, global order). Modern CPUs relax this for performance: x86 (TSO — Total Store Order) — allows store-load reordering (a store followed by a load to a different address can be reordered) but preserves store-store and load-load order. ARM — weakly ordered — most reorderings allowed (needs memory barriers for synchronization). The programmer uses memory barriers (fence instructions) to enforce ordering when needed (locks, lock-free algorithms). Without barriers, the hardware may reorder for performance, breaking naive synchronization. The consistency model is the contract between the hardware and the programmer — weaker models are faster but harder to program (requires careful barrier use or atomics with built-in ordering)." },
  { id: "coa37", category: "Performance", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is the difference between latency and throughput? Can you improve one without the other?", answer: "Latency: time for one operation to complete (e.g., 100ns for a memory access). Throughput: operations per unit time (e.g., 10 billion instructions/second). They're related but distinct: pipelining improves throughput (one instruction per cycle) without improving latency (each instruction still takes 5 cycles end-to-end). Superscalar improves both (multiple instructions per cycle). A cache miss increases latency (the access waits for memory) but may not affect throughput (out-of-order execution hides the latency by doing other work). A fast single-core CPU has low latency but limited throughput; a GPU has high throughput (thousands of threads) but high latency per thread. The tradeoff: deep pipelines increase throughput (higher clock) but increase latency (more stages) and branch misprediction penalty. Batching (process multiple items together) improves throughput but increases latency (wait for the batch). The goal depends on the workload: latency-sensitive (interactive, trading) vs throughput-sensitive (batch, analytics). Amdahl's law is about latency (speedup); Gustafson's is about throughput (scaled problems)." },
  { id: "coa38", category: "Performance", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is CPI (cycles per instruction)? How is it different from MIPS?", answer: "CPI: average clock cycles per instruction — the reciprocal of throughput. CPI = total cycles / total instructions. Ideal pipelined CPI = 1 (one instruction per cycle). Real CPI > 1 due to stalls (cache misses, branch mispredictions, resource conflicts). CPI is a measure of efficiency — lower is better. IPC (instructions per cycle) = 1/CPI — higher is better. Modern superscalar OoO CPUs have IPC 2-6 (multiple instructions per cycle). MIPS (Million Instructions Per Second): a raw throughput metric — MIPS = clock_speed / CPI / 10^6. MIPS is misleading: a RISC CPU may need more instructions for the same task (higher MIPS but not faster) — MIPS measures instruction count, not work done. CPI is more meaningful (it reflects the architecture's efficiency), but even CPI doesn't capture the full picture (instruction count, memory hierarchy, parallelism). Use benchmarks (SPEC, real workloads) for real performance comparison — synthetic metrics (MIPS, CPI) are hints, not conclusions. 'MIPS: Meaningless Indicator of Performance for Salesmen.'" },
  { id: "coa39", category: "ISA", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a calling convention? Why does it matter?", answer: "A calling convention defines how functions pass arguments, return values, and manage the stack — the contract between the caller and callee. It specifies: (1) Which registers hold arguments (first 6 in x86-64 System V: rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9; rest on the stack). (2) Which register holds the return value (rax). (3) Which registers are caller-saved (the caller must save/restore if it needs them across a call) vs callee-saved (the callee must preserve). (4) Who cleans the stack (caller or callee). (5) Stack alignment (16-byte on x86-64 before a call). Calling conventions matter for: ABI compatibility (linking code compiled by different compilers/languages), interop (C calling Fortran/assembly), and security (ROP gadgets depend on the convention). Mismatched conventions corrupt the stack or lose arguments. x86 has many (cdecl, stdcall, fastcall, thiscall); x86-64 standardized (System V on Unix, Microsoft x64 on Windows). ARM has AAPCS. The compiler follows the convention automatically; assembly/interop programmers must know it." },
  { id: "coa40", category: "Memory", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is the stack used for? What is the heap?", answer: "Stack: automatic storage for function frames — local variables, parameters, return addresses, saved registers. LIFO — allocated/deallocated by function call/return (push/pop the frame pointer). Fast (pointer bump — SP moves down on call, up on return). Limited size (1-8MB default — stack overflow on deep recursion or large locals). Thread-local (each thread has its own stack). The stack grows down (on most architectures) — SP decreases. Heap: dynamic allocation (malloc/new) — manual lifetime (free/delete). Large (limited by OS/virtual memory). Slower (bookkeeping, fragmentation). Shared across threads (with synchronization). The heap grows up (toward higher addresses) via brk/mmap. Use the stack for: short-lived, fixed-size data (locals). Use the heap for: long-lived, runtime-sized data (dynamic structures, large arrays). Stack is faster and automatic; heap is flexible but manual (leaks, fragmentation). In C, don't return a pointer to a stack variable (dangling — the frame is freed). The stack and heap grow toward each other in the address space." },
  { id: "coa41", category: "Pipelining", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a superscalar processor? How does it differ from a pipelined one?", answer: "Pipelined: one instruction enters the pipeline per cycle, one completes per cycle (in the steady state) — ILP via stage overlap. Superscalar: multiple instructions issued per cycle (2-8) — multiple ALUs, parallel execution units. This is a higher level of ILP — the CPU finds independent instructions and runs them simultaneously. Issue width: how many instructions per cycle (2-way, 4-way, 6-way). The challenge: finding enough independent instructions (data dependencies limit it) — solved by out-of-order execution (look ahead in a window), register renaming (eliminate false deps), and branch prediction (keep the pipeline full). VLIW (Very Long Instruction Word): the compiler bundles independent instructions into one wide word — the CPU issues them in parallel (no OoO hardware — simpler, but compiler must find the parallelism). Modern CPUs are superscalar + OoO (e.g., Intel issues 6-8 per cycle, IPC 4-6). GPUs are wide SIMD/superscalar (thousands of threads). Superscalar multiplies the pipelined throughput — but diminishing returns (dependencies, memory latency limit IPC)." },
  { id: "coa42", category: "Parallelism", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is SIMD? How does it differ from MIMD?", answer: "SIMD (Single Instruction Multiple Data): one instruction operates on multiple data elements simultaneously — a vector instruction. Example: ADD 8 floats in one instruction (AVX — 256-bit = 8 floats). Used for: graphics, signal processing, ML (matrix multiply), media codecs. The hardware has wide registers (128/256/512-bit) and vector ALUs. SIMD is data parallelism (same operation on different data). MIMD (Multiple Instruction Multiple Data): multiple independent processors (cores) each running their own instruction stream on their own data — a multi-core CPU. SIMD is one core doing parallel data ops; MIMD is multiple cores doing independent work. GPUs are SIMT (Single Instruction Multiple Threads) — a hybrid (a warp of threads executes the same instruction, but each thread has its own data and can diverge). SIMD is efficient for regular, data-parallel workloads (matrices, pixels); MIMD is general-purpose (different threads, different tasks). Modern CPUs use both: MIMD (multi-core) + SIMD (vector units within each core). Auto-vectorization (compiler) and intrinsics (manual) exploit SIMD." },
  { id: "coa43", category: "Cache", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a victim cache? What problem does it solve?", answer: "A victim cache is a small fully-associative cache that holds blocks evicted from the main (usually direct-mapped) cache. When a block is evicted from the L1 (due to a conflict miss), it goes to the victim cache. On a subsequent access, if the block is in the victim cache, it's promoted back to the L1 (and the evicted block goes to the victim cache). Purpose: reduce conflict misses in direct-mapped caches (where two hot blocks mapping to the same set thrash). The victim cache provides a 'second chance' for evicted blocks without the cost of full associativity. It's small (4-16 entries) and fully-associative (flexible). Originally proposed by Norman Jouppi (1990) for the direct-mapped MIPS R4000. Modern CPUs with set-associative L1s have fewer conflict misses, so victim caches are less common, but the concept survives in: victim buffers for TLBs, and the L2/L3 acting as a 'victim' for the L1 (exclusive caches — evicted L1 blocks go to L2, not re-fetched from memory)." },
  { id: "coa44", category: "Memory", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is a page table walk? Why is it expensive?", answer: "A page table walk is the process of translating a virtual address to a physical address by traversing the multi-level page table. For a 4-level table (x86-64): (1) Read the CR3 register (the top-level table's physical address). (2) Use bits 47-39 of the VA to index the PML4 — get the PDPT address. (3) Use bits 38-30 to index the PDPT — get the PD address. (4) Use bits 29-21 to index the PD — get the PT address. (5) Use bits 20-12 to index the PT — get the PTE (frame number + flags). Each step is a memory access (to the page table in RAM) — 4 memory accesses for a 4-level table, plus the actual data access (5 total). This is why the TLB is critical — without it, every memory access would be 5x slower. The walk is done by hardware (MMU page table walker) on modern CPUs (or by a software handler on MIPS/SPARC). Large pages (2MB) skip the last level (3 accesses); 1GB pages skip two (2 accesses). The walk also checks permissions (present, read/write, user/supervisor) — a violation causes a page fault (OS handles it)." },
  { id: "coa45", category: "Performance", difficulty: "medium", question: "What is the difference between a benchmark and a microbenchmark? What is SPEC?", answer: "A benchmark measures performance on a representative workload. A microbenchmark tests a specific operation in isolation (e.g., memory latency, branch prediction accuracy) — useful for diagnosing bottlenecks but not representative of real performance. A macrobenchmark (full application) reflects real-world performance. SPEC (Standard Performance Evaluation Corporation) benchmarks: industry-standard CPU benchmarks (SPEC CPU — integer and floating-point suites), with source code compiled and run; the result (SPECscore) is a geometric mean of normalized times. SPECint (integer — gcc, perl, AI), SPECfp (floating-point — physics, weather). SPEC is trusted because it's real code, standardized, and audited. Other benchmarks: Geekbench (cross-platform), Linpack (HPC — TOP500), MLPerf (AI). Pitfalls: optimizing for the benchmark (not real workloads), compiler flags (over-optimizing SPEC), and benchmark selection (a CPU good at SPEC may be bad at your workload). Always benchmark YOUR workload on YOUR hardware — synthetic scores are hints, not conclusions. 'Lies, damned lies, and benchmarks.'" },
];